    "10.9": [
      {"bad":"The team had a busy week. It was late, however, we continued. The work had to be finished. We finished the report, then we went home. Everyone was exhausted but pleased.","good":"The team had a busy week. It was late; however, we continued. The work had to be finished. We finished the report; then we went home. Everyone was exhausted but pleased.","explanation":"Use a semicolon before a conjunctive adverb or to join related clauses.","sentences":[{"bad":"The team had a busy week.","good":"The team had a busy week."},{"bad":"It was late, however, we continued.","good":"It was late; however, we continued."},{"bad":"The work had to be finished.","good":"The work had to be finished."},{"bad":"We finished the report, then we went home.","good":"We finished the report; then we went home."},{"bad":"Everyone was exhausted but pleased.","good":"Everyone was exhausted but pleased."}]},
      {"bad":"She loves outdoor sports. I have three hobbies swimming, running and reading. Each one keeps her active. She has one aim to run a marathon. She trains every morning.","good":"She loves outdoor sports. I have three hobbies: swimming, running and reading. Each one keeps her active. She has one aim: to run a marathon. She trains every morning.","explanation":"A colon introduces a list or an explanation.","sentences":[{"bad":"She loves outdoor sports.","good":"She loves outdoor sports."},{"bad":"I have three hobbies swimming, running and reading.","good":"I have three hobbies: swimming, running and reading."},{"bad":"Each one keeps her active.","good":"Each one keeps her active."},{"bad":"She has one aim to run a marathon.","good":"She has one aim: to run a marathon."},{"bad":"She trains every morning.","good":"She trains every morning."}]},
      {"bad":"The trip required careful planning. We needed three things, tickets, maps and sunscreen. The list was pinned to the wall. We packed for everything the weather was uncertain. In the end we used everything.","good":"The trip required careful planning. We needed three things: tickets, maps and sunscreen. The list was pinned to the wall. We packed for everything: the weather was uncertain. In the end we used everything.","explanation":"Use a colon before a list and a semicolon between related clauses.","sentences":[{"bad":"The trip required careful planning.","good":"The trip required careful planning."},{"bad":"We needed three things, tickets, maps and sunscreen.","good":"We needed three things: tickets, maps and sunscreen."},{"bad":"The list was pinned to the wall.","good":"The list was pinned to the wall."},{"bad":"We packed for everything the weather was uncertain.","good":"We packed for everything: the weather was uncertain."},{"bad":"In the end we used everything.","good":"In the end we used everything."}]},
      {"bad":"The recipe had a short list of ingredients. We needed two things flour and butter. The rest were already in the cupboard. The dough was ready, we shaped it at once. The biscuits smelled wonderful.","good":"The recipe had a short list of ingredients. We needed two things: flour and butter. The rest were already in the cupboard. The dough was ready; we shaped it at once. The biscuits smelled wonderful.","explanation":"A colon introduces the list; a semicolon joins the clauses.","sentences":[{"bad":"The recipe had a short list of ingredients.","good":"The recipe had a short list of ingredients."},{"bad":"We needed two things flour and butter.","good":"We needed two things: flour and butter."},{"bad":"The rest were already in the cupboard.","good":"The rest were already in the cupboard."},{"bad":"The dough was ready, we shaped it at once.","good":"The dough was ready; we shaped it at once."},{"bad":"The biscuits smelled wonderful.","good":"The biscuits smelled wonderful."}]},
      {"bad":"The manager called a meeting. There was one clear reason the budget. The team discussed it openly. The figures were clear, we had to save more. Everyone agreed on a plan.","good":"The manager called a meeting. There was one clear reason: the budget. The team discussed it openly. The figures were clear; we had to save more. Everyone agreed on a plan.","explanation":"Use a colon for a single explanation and a semicolon for related clauses.","sentences":[{"bad":"The manager called a meeting.","good":"The manager called a meeting."},{"bad":"There was one clear reason the budget.","good":"There was one clear reason: the budget."},{"bad":"The team discussed it openly.","good":"The team discussed it openly."},{"bad":"The figures were clear, we had to save more.","good":"The figures were clear; we had to save more."},{"bad":"Everyone agreed on a plan.","good":"Everyone agreed on a plan."}]}
