e":"1.1","name":"Regular noun plurals","example":"boxs","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Most English nouns form the plural by adding -s (or -es after s, x, z, ch, sh), so \"box\" becomes \"boxes\", not \"boxs\"."},{"constraint":"C-NOUN-FORM","rule":"1.2","name":"Irregular noun plurals","example":"childs","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Some nouns have irregular plurals that are not formed with -s: \"child → children\", \"mouse → mice\", \"man → men\"."},{"constraint":"C-POSSESSIVE-S","rule":"1.3","name":"Possessive ‘s / s’","example":"the boys toy","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A possessive shows ownership with 's (singular) or s' (plural already ending in -s): \"the boy's toy\", \"the boys' toys\"."},{"constraint":"C-VERB-ENDING","rule":"1.4","name":"Verb endings (-s, -ed, -ing)","example":"runing","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Verb endings must match their use: -s for third-person singular, -ed for past, -ing after be or as a gerund; spelling changes can apply (\"run → running\")."},{"constraint":"C-VERB-FORM","rule":"1.5","name":"Irregular past tense","example":"goed","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Many common verbs have irregular past-tense forms (\"go → went\", \"take → took\") that are not made by adding -ed."},{"constraint":"C-VBN-SIMPLE-PAST","rule":"1.6","name":"Irregular past participle","example":"had went","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"With have/had, the past participle is required; irregular verbs often differ from the past tense (\"had gone\", not \"had went\")."},{"constraint":"C-COMPARATIVE-FORM","rule":"1.7","name":"Comparative/superlative formation","example":"gooder, more tall","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Comparatives and superlatives use -er/-est for short adjectives and more/most for longer ones — not both (\"taller\" or \"more tall\", never \"more taller\")."},{"constraint":"C-ADVERB-FORM","rule":"1.8","name":"Adverb formation","example":"happyly","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"An adverb is normally formed from an adjective with -ly (\"happy → happily\"); some adjectives already end in -ly and stay unchanged."},{"constraint":"C-REFLEXIVE-FORM","rule":"1.9","name":"Reflexive forms","example":"hisself","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Reflexive pronouns end in -self/-selves and must match the subject (\"himself\", \"yourself\"), never non-standard forms like \"hisself\"."},{"constraint":"C-HOMOPHONE","rule":"1.10","name":"Homophones (its/it’s, their/there)","example":"Its raining","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Homophones sound alike but differ in meaning and spelling: \"its\" (possessive) vs \"it’s\" (it is), \"their\"/\"there\"/\"they’re\"."},{"constraint":"C-CONTRACTION","rule":"1.11","name":"Contractions","example":"dont","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"A contraction replaces missing letters with an apostrophe (\"do not → don’t\", \"I am → I’m\"); dropping the apostrophe changes the word."},{"constraint":"C-LEXICON-MALFORMED","rule":"1.12","name":"Spelling","example":"recieve","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"English spelling is not fully predictable, so some words must be learned (\"receive\", \"necessary\", \"separate\")."},{"constraint":"C-LEXICON-TAG-MISMATCH","rule":"1.12","name":"Spelling","example":"recieve","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"English spelling is not fully predictable, so some words must be learned (\"receive\", \"necessary\", \"separate\")."},{"constraint":"C-LEXICON-UNKNOWN","rule":"1.12","name":"Spelling","example":"recieve","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"English spelling is not fully predictable, so some words must be learned (\"receive\", \"necessary\", \"separate\")."},{"constraint":"C-SPELLING","rule":"1.12","name":"Spelling","example":"recieve","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"English spelling is not fully predictable, so some words must be learned (\"receive\", \"necessary\", \"separate\")."},{"constraint":"C-NUMERAL","rule":"1.13","name":"Numerals and measures","example":"two hundreds","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Numbers and measures have fixed patterns: hundred/thousand stay singular after a numeral (\"two hundred\", not \"two hundreds\"); units follow the noun."},{"constraint":"C-CLAUSE-SUBJECT","rule":"2.1","name":"Subject-verb, simple","example":"The boy go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A verb must agree in number with its subject: a singular subject takes a singular verb (\"The boy goes\"), a plural subject a plural verb."},{"constraint":"C-PREDICATE","rule":"2.1","name":"Subject-verb, simple","example":"The boy go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A verb must agree in number with its subject: a singular subject takes a singular verb (\"The boy goes\"), a plural subject a plural verb."},{"constraint":"C-SUBJECT-AGREEMENT","rule":"2.1","name":"Subject-verb, simple","example":"The boy go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A verb must agree in number with its subject: a singular subject takes a singular verb (\"The boy goes\"), a plural subject a plural verb."},{"constraint":"C-WAS-WERE","rule":"2.1","name":"Subject-verb, simple","example":"The boy go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A verb must agree in number with its subject: a singular subject takes a singular verb (\"The boy goes\"), a plural subject a plural verb."},{"constraint":"C-SUBJ-AGREE-XP","rule":"2.2","name":"Subject-verb across intervening phrase","example":"The boy with the bags go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Agreement ignores phrases between the subject and verb, so the verb matches the head noun, not the nearest noun (\"The boy with the bags goes\")."},{"constraint":"C-COORD-SUBJ-AGREE","rule":"2.3","name":"Coordinated subjects (and / or / nor)","example":"Tom or Ann are","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"With subjects joined by \"and\" the verb is plural; with \"or\"/\"nor\" it agrees with the nearer subject (\"Tom or Ann is\")."},{"constraint":"C-COORDINATOR","rule":"2.3","name":"Coordinated subjects (and / or / nor)","example":"Tom or Ann are","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"With subjects joined by \"and\" the verb is plural; with \"or\"/\"nor\" it agrees with the nearer subject (\"Tom or Ann is\")."},{"constraint":"C-EXISTENTIAL","rule":"2.4","name":"Existential there","example":"There is books","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"In \"there is/are\", the verb agrees with the following noun, not with \"there\" (\"There are books\")."},{"constraint":"C-EXISTENTIAL-VERB","rule":"2.4","name":"Existential there","example":"There is books","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"In \"there is/are\", the verb agrees with the following noun, not with \"there\" (\"There are books\")."},{"constraint":"C-COLLECTIVE","rule":"2.5","name":"Collective and notional nouns","example":"The team are","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Collective and notional nouns can take a singular or plural verb depending on whether the group is treated as one unit or several members."},{"constraint":"","rule":"2.6","name":"Questions and inversion","example":"Where is the books?","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"In questions the verb agrees with the subject and appears after the interrogative word (\"Where are the books?\")."},{"constraint":"C-NP-RELATIVE","rule":"2.7","name":"Relative clauses","example":"the boys who plays","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Inside a relative clause the verb agrees with the relative pronoun’s antecedent (\"the boys who play\", not \"who plays\")."},{"constraint":"C-RELATIVE-AGREE","rule":"2.7","name":"Relative clauses","example":"the boys who plays","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Inside a relative clause the verb agrees with the relative pronoun’s antecedent (\"the boys who play\", not \"who plays\")."},{"constraint":"C-RELATIVE-CLAUSE","rule":"2.7","name":"Relative clauses","example":"the boys who plays","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Inside a relative clause the verb agrees with the relative pronoun’s antecedent (\"the boys who play\", not \"who plays\")."},{"constraint":"C-INDEF-AGREE","rule":"2.8","name":"Indefinite pronouns","example":"Everyone have","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Indefinite pronouns like everyone, somebody, each take a singular verb (\"Everyone has\")."},{"constraint":"C-INDEFINITE-AGREE","rule":"2.8","name":"Indefinite pronouns","example":"Everyone have","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Indefinite pronouns like everyone, somebody, each take a singular verb (\"Everyone has\")."},{"constraint":"C-NUMBER-AGREE","rule":"2.9","name":"Quantified subjects","example":"A number of / Each of the boys are","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Quantified subjects agree according to the head: \"each/a number of\" patterns determine singular or plural (\"Each of the boys is\")."},{"constraint":"C-DETERMINER-AGREEMENT","rule":"2.10","name":"Determiner-noun number","example":"these book","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Demonstratives must match noun number: \"this/that\" with singular, \"these/those\" with plural (\"these books\")."},{"constraint":"C-QUANTIFIER","rule":"2.11","name":"Quantifier-noun","example":"less books, much books","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Quantifiers pair with the right noun type: \"much/little\" with uncountable, \"many/few\" with countable (\"less water\", \"fewer books\")."},{"constraint":"C-QUANTIFIER-CLASS","rule":"2.11","name":"Quantifier-noun","example":"less books, much books","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Quantifiers pair with the right noun type: \"much/little\" with uncountable, \"many/few\" with countable (\"less water\", \"fewer books\")."},{"constraint":"C-PRON-ANTECEDENT","rule":"2.12","name":"Pronoun-antecedent","example":"The girls lost her bag","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"A pronoun must match its antecedent in number and gender (\"The girls lost their bags\")."},{"constraint":"C-CORRELATIVE-AGREE","rule":"2.13","name":"Correlatives","example":"Either he or I am","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"With correlatives (either...or, neither...nor) the verb agrees with the nearer subject (\"Either he or I am\")."},{"constraint":"C-REFLEXIVE-ANTECEDENT","rule":"2.14","name":"Reflexive-antecedent","example":"She hurt himself","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A reflexive pronoun must refer back to the subject of the clause (\"She hurt herself\")."},{"constraint":"C-ARTICLE-MISMATCH","rule":"3.1","name":"a/an choice","example":"a apple","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Use \"an\" before a vowel sound and \"a\" before a consonant sound (\"an apple\", \"a house\")."},{"constraint":"C-ARTICLE","rule":"3.2","name":"Missing article","example":"I saw dog","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Singular countable nouns need a determiner in most contexts (\"I saw a dog\"), unless the meaning is generic or indefinite plural."},{"constraint":"C-UNNECESSARY-ARTICLE","rule":"3.3","name":"Unnecessary article","example":"She plays the football","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Do not add an article before activities, sports, or generic plurals (\"She plays football\", not \"the football\")."},{"constraint":"C-FIRST-MENTION","rule":"3.4","name":"Definite vs indefinite","example":"I saw the dog (first mention)","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Use \"the\" for something already known or specific, and \"a/an\" for first mention or one of many."},{"constraint":"C-DET-STACK","rule":"3.5","name":"Stacked determiners","example":"the my book","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Only one central determiner is allowed per noun phrase — do not stack them (\"my book\", not \"the my book\")."},{"constraint":"C-DETERMINER","rule":"3.6","name":"Determiner with no noun","example":"the my","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A determiner must modify a noun; it cannot stand alone without one (\"the my\" is incomplete)."},{"constraint":"C-HEAD-NOUN","rule":"3.6","name":"Determiner with no noun","example":"the my","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A determiner must modify a noun; it cannot stand alone without one (\"the my\" is incomplete)."},{"constraint":"C-SOME-ANY","rule":"3.7","name":"some/any","example":"I don’t have some money","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Use \"some\" mainly in positive statements and \"any\" in questions and negatives (\"I don’t have any money\")."},{"constraint":"C-DET-NOUN-AGREE","rule":"3.8","name":"each/every/all/whole/other/another","example":"another books","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Distributive determiners match noun number and meaning: \"each/every\" + singular, \"another\" + singular, \"other/all\" + plural."},{"constraint":"C-UNCOUNTABLE","rule":"4.1","name":"Countable vs uncountable","example":"informations, an advice","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Uncountable nouns have no plural and take no \"a/an\" (\"information\", \"advice\"), unlike countable nouns (\"a fact\", \"two facts\")."},{"constraint":"C-NOUN-ADJUNCT","rule":"4.2","name":"Noun as modifier (number)","example":"a ten-years plan","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"When a noun modifies another noun it usually stays singular (\"a ten-year plan\", \"a shoe shop\")."},{"constraint":"C-NOUN-MODIFIER-NUMBER","rule":"4.2","name":"Noun as modifier (number)","example":"a ten-years plan","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"When a noun modifies another noun it usually stays singular (\"a ten-year plan\", \"a shoe shop\")."},{"constraint":"C-NP-COMPOUND","rule":"4.2","name":"Noun as modifier (number)","example":"a ten-years plan","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"When a noun modifies another noun it usually stays singular (\"a ten-year plan\", \"a shoe shop\")."},{"constraint":"C-PLURAL-ONLY","rule":"4.3","name":"Plural-only / singular -s nouns","example":"a scissors, the news are","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Some nouns are plural-only (\"scissors\", \"trousers\") and some look plural but are singular (\"the news is\")."},{"constraint":"C-PREP-PRON-CASE","rule":"5.1","name":"Case, simple","example":"Me go, between you and I","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Pronouns change form by grammatical role: subject (\"I\", \"he\") vs object (\"me\", \"him\"), including after prepositions and \"between\"."},{"constraint":"C-PRONOUN-CASE","rule":"5.1","name":"Case, simple","example":"Me go, between you and I","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Pronouns change form by grammatical role: subject (\"I\", \"he\") vs object (\"me\", \"him\"), including after prepositions and \"between\"."},{"constraint":"C-COORD-CASE","rule":"5.2","name":"Case in coordination and comparison","example":"Me and him went, taller than I","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"In coordinated or compared phrases, keep the pronoun in the case the role requires (\"He and I went\", not \"Me and him went\")."},{"constraint":"C-PRON-AMBIG","rule":"5.3","name":"Ambiguous reference","example":"Tom told Jim he...","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"A pronoun should refer clearly to one antecedent; if two nouns could be meant, the reference is ambiguous and should be rewritten."},{"constraint":"C-RELATIVE-HEAD","rule":"5.4","name":"Relative pronoun choice","example":"the man which","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Choose the relative pronoun by the antecedent: \"who\" for people, \"which\" for things, \"that\" for either in restrictive clauses."},{"constraint":"C-RELATIVE-NOUN","rule":"5.4","name":"Relative pronoun choice","example":"the man which","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Choose the relative pronoun by the antecedent: \"who\" for people, \"which\" for things, \"that\" for either in restrictive clauses."},{"constraint":"C-RELATIVE-POSSESSIVE","rule":"5.4","name":"Relative pronoun choice","example":"the man which","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Choose the relative pronoun by the antecedent: \"who\" for people, \"which\" for things, \"that\" for either in restrictive clauses."},{"constraint":"C-RELATIVE-PRONOUN","rule":"5.4","name":"Relative pronoun choice","example":"the man which","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Choose the relative pronoun by the antecedent: \"who\" for people, \"which\" for things, \"that\" for either in restrictive clauses."},{"constraint":"C-DUMMY-IT","rule":"5.5","name":"Dummy it/there","example":"Is raining","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Dummy \"it\"/\"there\" must be present where English requires a subject (\"It is raining\", not \"Is raining\")."},{"constraint":"C-POSSESSIVE","rule":"5.6","name":"Possessive pronoun vs determiner","example":"This is my","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A possessive determiner (\"my\") needs a following noun; a possessive pronoun (\"mine\") stands alone (\"This is mine\", not \"This is my\")."},{"constraint":"C-REFLEXIVE-SUBJECT","rule":"5.7","name":"Reflexive vs ordinary pronoun","example":"Myself am happy","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A reflexive pronoun cannot be the subject of a finite clause; use an ordinary pronoun (\"I am happy\", not \"Myself am happy\")."},{"constraint":"C-AUX-COMPLEMENT","rule":"6.1","name":"Auxiliary + verb form","example":"She can goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After an auxiliary or modal, use the required form: base form after modals (\"can go\"), past participle after have, -ing after be."},{"constraint":"C-AUX-FORM","rule":"6.1","name":"Auxiliary + verb form","example":"She can goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After an auxiliary or modal, use the required form: base form after modals (\"can go\"), past participle after have, -ing after be."},{"constraint":"C-AUXILIARY","rule":"6.1","name":"Auxiliary + verb form","example":"She can goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After an auxiliary or modal, use the required form: base form after modals (\"can go\"), past participle after have, -ing after be."},{"constraint":"C-MODAL-VERB-FORM","rule":"6.1","name":"Auxiliary + verb form","example":"She can goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After an auxiliary or modal, use the required form: base form after modals (\"can go\"), past participle after have, -ing after be."},{"constraint":"C-HEAD-VERB","rule":"6.2","name":"Finite main verb","example":"She going to school","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A main clause needs a finite verb (tensed and agreeing), not a bare -ing or infinitive with no auxiliary (\"She goes to school\")."},{"constraint":"C-TENSE-FINITE","rule":"6.2","name":"Finite main verb","example":"She going to school","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A main clause needs a finite verb (tensed and agreeing), not a bare -ing or infinitive with no auxiliary (\"She goes to school\")."},{"constraint":"C-COPULA","rule":"6.3","name":"Missing verb / copula","example":"She very happy","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"English clauses need a verb, including the copula \"be\"; adjectives cannot stand alone as predicates (\"She is very happy\")."},{"constraint":"C-PLEASE-TO","rule":"6.3","name":"Missing verb / copula","example":"She very happy","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"English clauses need a verb, including the copula \"be\"; adjectives cannot stand alone as predicates (\"She is very happy\")."},{"constraint":"C-PASSIVE","rule":"6.4","name":"Passive formation","example":"The house build","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A passive is formed with be + past participle (\"The house was built\"), not the bare base form."},{"constraint":"C-INTRANSITIVE-PASSIVE","rule":"6.5","name":"Intransitive passive","example":"It was happened","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Intransitive verbs (happen, arrive, sleep) have no passive because they take no object (\"It happened\", not \"It was happened\")."},{"constraint":"C-STATIVE-PROG","rule":"6.6","name":"Stative verbs in progressive","example":"I am knowing","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Stative verbs (know, like, believe) describe states and are not normally used in the progressive (\"I know\", not \"I am knowing\")."},{"constraint":"C-STATIVE-PROGRESSIVE","rule":"6.6","name":"Stative verbs in progressive","example":"I am knowing","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Stative verbs (know, like, believe) describe states and are not normally used in the progressive (\"I know\", not \"I am knowing\")."},{"constraint":"C-TENSE-CONSISTENCY","rule":"6.7","name":"Tense consistency","example":"He said he will come","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Keep tense and logic consistent across a sentence or narrative (\"He said he would come\")."},{"constraint":"C-TRANSITIVITY","rule":"6.8","name":"Transitivity","example":"discuss about, arrived the station","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Verbs take the complements their meaning allows; some need no preposition (\"discuss\", not \"discuss about\")."},{"constraint":"C-GERUND-INFIN","rule":"6.9","name":"Gerund vs infinitive","example":"enjoy to swim","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Some verbs are followed by a gerund, some by an infinitive, and some by both with a change in meaning (\"enjoy swimming\")."},{"constraint":"C-GERUND-INFINITIVE","rule":"6.9","name":"Gerund vs infinitive","example":"enjoy to swim","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Some verbs are followed by a gerund, some by an infinitive, and some by both with a change in meaning (\"enjoy swimming\")."},{"constraint":"C-INFINITIVAL-COMPLEMENT","rule":"6.10","name":"to + base form","example":"to goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After the infinitive marker \"to\" use the base form (\"to go\", not \"to goes\")."},{"constraint":"C-INFINITIVAL-MARKER","rule":"6.10","name":"to + base form","example":"to goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After the infinitive marker \"to\" use the base form (\"to go\", not \"to goes\")."},{"constraint":"C-TO-BASE","rule":"6.10","name":"to + base form","example":"to goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"After the infinitive marker \"to\" use the base form (\"to go\", not \"to goes\")."},{"constraint":"C-PARTICLE","rule":"6.11","name":"Phrasal verbs","example":"pick up it","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"A phrasal verb keeps its particle next to it, and a pronoun object must go between verb and particle (\"pick it up\")."},{"constraint":"C-PHRASAL-ORDER","rule":"6.11","name":"Phrasal verbs","example":"pick up it","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"A phrasal verb keeps its particle next to it, and a pronoun object must go between verb and particle (\"pick it up\")."},{"constraint":"C-MODAL-STACK","rule":"6.12","name":"Modals and semi-modals","example":"must to go, use to go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Modals take the bare base form and add no \"-s\" or \"-ed\" (\"must go\", not \"must to go\")."},{"constraint":"C-MODAL-TO","rule":"6.12","name":"Modals and semi-modals","example":"must to go, use to go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Modals take the bare base form and add no \"-s\" or \"-ed\" (\"must go\", not \"must to go\")."},{"constraint":"C-QUESTION-INVERSION","rule":"6.13","name":"Question formation","example":"Where you are going?","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Yes/no and wh- questions invert the subject and auxiliary (\"Where are you going?\")."},{"constraint":"C-DOUBLE-NEGATIVE","rule":"6.14","name":"Negation","example":"I don’t have no money","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Standard English uses a single negative; use \"any\" rather than a second \"no\" (\"I don’t have any money\")."},{"constraint":"C-IMPERATIVE-VERB","rule":"6.15","name":"Imperatives","example":"Don’t goes","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Imperatives use the base form with no subject and no \"-s\" (\"Go!\", not \"Goes!\")."},{"constraint":"C-CONDITIONAL-WILL","rule":"6.16","name":"Conditionals","example":"If I will go","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"In real conditionals use present tense in the if-clause, not \"will\" (\"If I go, I will see him\")."},{"constraint":"C-SUBJUNCTIVE","rule":"6.17","name":"Subjunctive and wish","example":"I wish I was","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"After \"wish\" and in the subjunctive, use the past or base form for hypotheticals (\"I wish I were here\")."},{"constraint":"C-REPORTED-SPEECH","rule":"6.18","name":"Reported speech","example":"She said me","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Reported speech shifts tense and uses \"tell + person\" or \"say + clause\" (\"She told me\", not \"She said me\")."},{"constraint":"C-CAUSATIVE-TO","rule":"6.19","name":"Causatives and perception verbs","example":"made me to cry","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Causative and perception verbs take specific structures: \"make/let + base\", \"have/get + past participle\", \"see + base/-ing\"."},{"constraint":"C-CLAUSAL-COMPLEMENT","rule":"6.20","name":"Verb + clause patterns","example":"suggest him to go","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Different verbs select different clause patterns (\"suggest going\", \"advise someone to go\"), so the complement must match the verb."},{"constraint":"C-VERB-CLAUSE-PATTERN","rule":"6.20","name":"Verb + clause patterns","example":"suggest him to go","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Different verbs select different clause patterns (\"suggest going\", \"advise someone to go\"), so the complement must match the verb."},{"constraint":"C-ADJ-ADV","rule":"7.1","name":"Adjective vs adverb","example":"runs quick","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. Use the adverb after an action verb (\"runs quickly\")."},{"constraint":"C-HEAD-ADJECTIVE","rule":"7.1","name":"Adjective vs adverb","example":"runs quick","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. Use the adverb after an action verb (\"runs quickly\")."},{"constraint":"C-PREDICATE-ADJECTIVE","rule":"7.1","name":"Adjective vs adverb","example":"runs quick","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. Use the adverb after an action verb (\"runs quickly\")."},{"constraint":"C-SMALL-CLAUSE-PREDICATE","rule":"7.1","name":"Adjective vs adverb","example":"runs quick","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. Use the adverb after an action verb (\"runs quickly\")."},{"constraint":"C-ADJ-ORDER","rule":"7.2","name":"Adjective order","example":"red big car","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"When several adjectives stack, English fixes their order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose."},{"constraint":"C-ADVERB-PLACEMENT","rule":"7.3","name":"Adverb placement","example":"I like very much it","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adverbs of frequency and degree have preferred positions and usually cannot split a verb and its object."},{"constraint":"C-ADVERBIAL","rule":"7.3","name":"Adverb placement","example":"I like very much it","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adverbs of frequency and degree have preferred positions and usually cannot split a verb and its object."},{"constraint":"C-ADVERBIAL-COMPLEMENT","rule":"7.3","name":"Adverb placement","example":"I like very much it","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adverbs of frequency and degree have preferred positions and usually cannot split a verb and its object."},{"constraint":"C-HEAD-ADVERBIAL","rule":"7.3","name":"Adverb placement","example":"I like very much it","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Adverbs of frequency and degree have preferred positions and usually cannot split a verb and its object."},{"constraint":"C-PARTICIPIAL","rule":"7.4","name":"Participial adjectives","example":"I am boring","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Participial adjectives differ: \"-ing\" describes the cause (\"boring\"), \"-ed\" describes the feeler (\"bored\")."},{"constraint":"C-PARTICIPIAL-ADJ","rule":"7.4","name":"Participial adjectives","example":"I am boring","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Participial adjectives differ: \"-ing\" describes the cause (\"boring\"), \"-ed\" describes the feeler (\"bored\")."},{"constraint":"C-PARTICIPLE-MODIFIER","rule":"7.4","name":"Participial adjectives","example":"I am boring","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Participial adjectives differ: \"-ing\" describes the cause (\"boring\"), \"-ed\" describes the feeler (\"bored\")."},{"constraint":"C-INTENSIFIER","rule":"7.5","name":"Intensifiers and degree","example":"enough big, too much good","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Degree words combine with the right adjective and position: \"big enough\", \"very good\", \"too big\" — not \"enough big\"."},{"constraint":"C-COMPARATIVE-DOUBLE","rule":"7.6","name":"Comparative structures","example":"more taller, as tall than","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Form comparatives once: either -er/-est or more/most, and use \"than\" for comparison (\"taller than\")."},{"constraint":"C-COMPARATIVE-STRUCT","rule":"7.6","name":"Comparative structures","example":"more taller, as tall than","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Form comparatives once: either -er/-est or more/most, and use \"than\" for comparison (\"taller than\")."},{"constraint":"C-MOD-POS","rule":"7.7","name":"Modifier position","example":"the car red","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"English puts most modifiers before the noun; reversing the order (\"the car red\") is ungrammatical."},{"constraint":"C-NP-POSTMODIFIER","rule":"7.7","name":"Modifier position","example":"the car red","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"English puts most modifiers before the noun; reversing the order (\"the car red\") is ungrammatical."},{"constraint":"C-HEAD-PREPOSITION","rule":"8.1","name":"Wrong preposition","example":"depends in","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"The preposition a verb or adjective takes is fixed by convention (\"depend on\", not \"depend in\")."},{"constraint":"C-PREP-CHOICE","rule":"8.1","name":"Wrong preposition","example":"depends in","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"The preposition a verb or adjective takes is fixed by convention (\"depend on\", not \"depend in\")."},{"constraint":"C-WRONG-PREPOSITION","rule":"8.1","name":"Wrong preposition","example":"depends in","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"The preposition a verb or adjective takes is fixed by convention (\"depend on\", not \"depend in\")."},{"constraint":"C-MISSING-PREP","rule":"8.2","name":"Missing preposition","example":"listen music","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Some verbs require a preposition to introduce their object (\"listen to music\", not \"listen music\")."},{"constraint":"C-EXTRA-PREP","rule":"8.3","name":"Unnecessary preposition","example":"discuss about","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Some verbs already include the meaning of the preposition, so adding one is wrong (\"discuss the plan\")."},{"constraint":"C-ADJ-PREP","rule":"8.4","name":"Adjective + preposition","example":"interested on","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Adjective + preposition pairs are fixed (\"interested in\", \"good at\", \"afraid of\")."},{"constraint":"C-ADJUNCT","rule":"8.5","name":"Time and place (in/on/at)","example":"on 5 pm","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Time and place prepositions follow set patterns: \"at\" a point, \"on\" a day/date, \"in\" a period/area."},{"constraint":"C-PREP-OBJECT","rule":"8.5","name":"Time and place (in/on/at)","example":"on 5 pm","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Time and place prepositions follow set patterns: \"at\" a point, \"on\" a day/date, \"in\" a period/area."},{"constraint":"C-TIME-PLACE-PREP","rule":"8.5","name":"Time and place (in/on/at)","example":"on 5 pm","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Time and place prepositions follow set patterns: \"at\" a point, \"on\" a day/date, \"in\" a period/area."},{"constraint":"C-PREP-GERUND","rule":"8.6","name":"Preposition + gerund","example":"look forward to see","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A preposition is followed by a noun or a gerund (\"look forward to seeing\"), not a bare infinitive."},{"constraint":"C-FRAGMENT","rule":"9.1","name":"Fragment","example":"Because I was late.","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A sentence needs at least one independent clause; a subordinate clause alone is a fragment (\"Because I was late\" cannot stand alone)."},{"constraint":"C-ROOT","rule":"9.1","name":"Fragment","example":"Because I was late.","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A sentence needs at least one independent clause; a subordinate clause alone is a fragment (\"Because I was late\" cannot stand alone)."},{"constraint":"C-MISSING-SUBJECT","rule":"9.2","name":"Missing subject","example":"Went to school.","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A finite clause needs a subject; leaving it out produces a fragment (\"I went to school\")."},{"constraint":"C-RUNON","rule":"9.3","name":"Run-on / fused sentence","example":"I like tea she likes coffee","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Two independent clauses cannot sit side by side without a conjunction or punctuation (\"I like tea, but she likes coffee\")."},{"constraint":"C-COMMA-SPLICE","rule":"9.4","name":"Comma splice","example":"I came, I saw.","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A comma alone cannot join two independent clauses; use a conjunction, a semicolon, or a full stop."},{"constraint":"C-DOUBLE-SUBJECT","rule":"9.5","name":"Double subject","example":"My brother he...","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Do not give the subject twice with a pronoun (\"My brother went\", not \"My brother he went\")."},{"constraint":"C-WORD-ORDER","rule":"9.6","name":"Word order in statements","example":"Yesterday went I","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Statements use subject-before-verb order; inverted order belongs only to questions (\"Yesterday I went\")."},{"constraint":"C-EXTRA-WORD","rule":"9.7","name":"Missing or extra word","example":"She is very the happy","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"A clause should carry exactly the words it needs; extras or omissions break the structure (\"She is very happy\")."},{"constraint":"C-MISPLACED-MODIFIER","rule":"9.8","name":"Parallel structure","example":"to run, swimming","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Items in a list or comparison must share the same grammatical form (\"to run and to swim\", not \"to run and swimming\")."},{"constraint":"C-PARALLEL","rule":"9.8","name":"Parallel structure","example":"to run, swimming","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Items in a list or comparison must share the same grammatical form (\"to run and to swim\", not \"to run and swimming\")."},{"constraint":"C-CONJUNCTION-PAIR","rule":"9.9","name":"Conjunction pairing","example":"Although..., but...","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Correlative conjunctions come in fixed pairs; do not add a second connector (\"Although X, Y\" without \"but\")."},{"constraint":"C-NP-REDUCED-RELATIVE","rule":"9.10","name":"Relative clause formation","example":"the man who I saw him","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A relative clause already contains the role of the antecedent, so do not repeat it with a pronoun (\"the man who I saw\")."},{"constraint":"C-RESUMPTIVE-RELATIVE","rule":"9.10","name":"Relative clause formation","example":"the man who I saw him","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"A relative clause already contains the role of the antecedent, so do not repeat it with a pronoun (\"the man who I saw\")."},{"constraint":"C-DANGLING","rule":"9.11","name":"Dangling / misplaced modifier","example":"Walking home, the rain started","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"A modifier at the start of a sentence should describe the subject of the main clause (\"Walking home, I saw the rain start\")."},{"constraint":"C-REDUNDANCY","rule":"9.12","name":"Redundancy","example":"return back","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Avoid redundant words that repeat meaning (\"return\", not \"return back\")."},{"constraint":"C-INDIRECT-Q-ORDER","rule":"9.13","name":"Indirect question word order","example":"I wonder where is he","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"An embedded question keeps statement word order (\"I wonder where he is\")."},{"constraint":"C-INDIRECT-WH-INVERSION","rule":"9.13","name":"Indirect question word order","example":"I wonder where is he","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"An embedded question keeps statement word order (\"I wonder where he is\")."},{"constraint":"C-CLAUSAL-ADVERBIAL","rule":"9.14","name":"Clause linking","example":"because to see you","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Link clauses with the structure each connector requires (\"because I wanted to see you\")."},{"constraint":"C-CLAUSE-LINKING","rule":"9.14","name":"Clause linking","example":"because to see you","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Link clauses with the structure each connector requires (\"because I wanted to see you\")."},{"constraint":"C-CLAUSE-PREDICATE","rule":"9.14","name":"Clause linking","example":"because to see you","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Link clauses with the structure each connector requires (\"because I wanted to see you\")."},{"constraint":"C-COMPLEMENTIZER","rule":"9.14","name":"Clause linking","example":"because to see you","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Link clauses with the structure each connector requires (\"because I wanted to see you\")."},{"constraint":"C-DIRECT-OBJECT","rule":"9.15","name":"Object order","example":"gave to me a book","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Give the direct and indirect object in the standard order, or use the right preposition (\"give me a book\", \"give a book to me\")."},{"constraint":"C-OBJECT-ORDER","rule":"9.15","name":"Object order","example":"gave to me a book","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Give the direct and indirect object in the standard order, or use the right preposition (\"give me a book\", \"give a book to me\")."},{"constraint":"C-SMALL-CLAUSE","rule":"9.15","name":"Object order","example":"gave to me a book","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Give the direct and indirect object in the standard order, or use the right preposition (\"give me a book\", \"give a book to me\")."},{"constraint":"C-CAPITALIZATION","rule":"10.1","name":"Sentence-initial capital","example":"the boy runs","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Begin every sentence with a capital letter."},{"constraint":"C-SENTENCE-CAPITAL","rule":"10.1","name":"Sentence-initial capital","example":"the boy runs","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Begin every sentence with a capital letter."},{"constraint":"C-COMMA-OMISSION","rule":"10.2","name":"Proper nouns, “I”, days, months","example":"i am, monday","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Capitalise proper nouns, the pronoun \"I\", and the names of days and months."},{"constraint":"C-PROPER-NOUN","rule":"10.2","name":"Proper nouns, “I”, days, months","example":"i am, monday","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Capitalise proper nouns, the pronoun \"I\", and the names of days and months."},{"constraint":"C-TERMINAL-PUNCT","rule":"10.3","name":"Terminal punctuation","example":"missing period/question mark","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"End a declarative or imperative sentence with a full stop, a question with \"?\" and a strong exclamation with \"!\"."},{"constraint":"C-INTRO-COMMA","rule":"10.4","name":"Commas","example":"series, introductory, clauses","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Commas separate list items, mark introductory phrases, and set off non-essential clauses — but they cannot join two main clauses."},{"constraint":"C-APOSTROPHE","rule":"10.5","name":"Apostrophes","example":"the 1990’s","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Apostrophes mark possession and contraction, not ordinary plurals (\"the 1990s\")."},{"constraint":"C-QUOTATION","rule":"10.6","name":"Quotation and dialogue","example":"mismatched marks","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Quotation marks must be opened and closed around the exact words, with punctuation placed correctly."},{"constraint":"C-HYPHEN","rule":"10.7","name":"Hyphens and dashes","example":"a well known author","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Use hyphens to join compound modifiers before a noun (\"a well-known author\") and dashes for breaks in a sentence."},{"constraint":"C-PUNCT","rule":"10.8","name":"Colons and semicolons","example":"misuse","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Use a colon to introduce a list or explanation and a semicolon to join closely related independent clauses."},{"constraint":"C-SPACING-REPEAT","rule":"10.9","name":"Spacing and repeats","example":"the the, space before comma","method":"METHOD_T","layer":"T","concept":"Use single spaces between words and avoid accidental repeated words (\"the the\")."},{"constraint":"C-NUMERAL-FORMAT","rule":"10.10","name":"Numbers, dates, abbreviations","example":"inconsistent format","method":"METHOD_L","layer":"T","concept":"Follow consistent conventions for numbers, dates, and abbreviations within a text."},{"constraint":"C-CONFUSABLE","rule":"11.1","name":"Confusable words","example":"affect/effect, lose/loose","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Confusable words sound or look alike but differ in meaning or part of speech (\"affect\" = verb, \"effect\" = noun)."},{"constraint":"C-COLLOCATION","rule":"11.2","name":"Collocations","example":"do a decision","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Collocations are word partnerships fixed by convention (\"make a decision\", not \"do a decision\")."},{"constraint":"C-TENSE-SINCE","rule":"11.3","name":"Tense-time agreement","example":"Yesterday I go","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"The tense must match the time expressed by adverbs such as \"yesterday\" (\"Yesterday I went\")."},{"constraint":"C-TENSE-TIME","rule":"11.3","name":"Tense-time agreement","example":"Yesterday I go","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"The tense must match the time expressed by adverbs such as \"yesterday\" (\"Yesterday I went\")."},{"constraint":"C-SELECTIONAL","rule":"11.4","name":"Selectional restrictions","example":"The book eats","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"A verb’s meaning restricts what can be its subject or object (\"The book eats\" is impossible)."},{"constraint":"C-PP-ATTACH","rule":"11.5","name":"Structural ambiguity","example":"PP-attachment","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Some sentences have more than one possible structure; the intended meaning should be made explicit."},{"constraint":"C-REGISTER","rule":"11.6","name":"Register","example":"slang in formal writing","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Register should match the situation: slang and casual forms do not belong in formal writing."},{"constraint":"C-L1-INTERFERENCE","rule":"11.7","name":"L1 interference / false friends","example":"I am agree","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"First-language patterns can produce errors in English (\"I am agree\" from \"estoy de acuerdo\"); use the English structure."},{"constraint":"C-CONNECTOR","rule":"11.8","name":"Connector misuse","example":"I was tired, however I slept","method":"METHOD_C","layer":"T","concept":"Connectors express a specific logical relation, so the one chosen must match the meaning (\"I was tired, so I slept\")."},{"constraint":"C-UNLISTED","rule":"","name":"","example":"","method":"","layer":"T","concept":""}]}]}
