CHAPTER III
RESEARCH METHODOLOGY
	This chapter presents the methodology, system design, algorithms, and processes used to develop SynTutor. It describes the use of Agile-Scrum methodology in creating a sophisticated linguistics tool. The chapter also discussed the conceptual framework, data gathering procedures, and algorithms implemented for the generation of syntax trees and the Explainable AI (XAI) component, namely neural constituency parsing, recursive-descent parse-string decoding, deterministic constraint-matrix evaluation, positive-evidence lexical licensing, and the hybrid acceptability-detection and correction-search channels. Ultimately, how the program will be validated will be discussed to ensure that the system is reliable for its intended users and meets aligned research standards.
3.1 SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT METHODOLOGY
	In integrating NLP with deterministic linguistic reasoning, SynTutor is developed with an iterative and adaptive approach, as required by its complex computational requirements. The researchers utilizes the Agile-Scrum methodology to create the Syntutor linguistic tool. This methodology provides a dynamic framework that involves continuous testing, immediate integration based on feedback, and the rapid-paced refinement of both graphical user interface (GUI) and the system’s engineering.

3.1.1 The Agile-Scrum Methodology
	Figure 3.1.1 Agile-Scrum Methodology


To iteratively construct SynTutor, the study adopts the Agile-Scrum methodology illustrated in Figure 3.1.1. The methodology follows a continuous pipeline that takes the project into cyclical, rapid development iterations known as sprints to produce the web application. The process begins by establishing the project's foundational scope, which defines the project boundaries, target domain, and core system objectives. These requirements are organized into the Product Backlog, serving as the centralized prioritized repository of all features, technical tasks, and definition of rules, including the NLP pipeline (spaCy tokenization plus the benepar neural constituency parser), the recursive-descent parse-string decoder, the deterministic Linguistic Constraint Matrix, the positive-evidence licensing layer, the hybrid acceptability detector and correction-search channel, and the interactive syntax-tree and practice-quiz interfaces. Following the backlog creation, the Design stage focuses on the system architecture, UI/UX wireframing for the website's interface of the tree renderer, state machine logic, and the database schemas for authentication.
Once the initial scope and design are established, development enters the iterative Scrum lifecycle. Each cycle begins with a sprint planning meeting where the research team selects priority tasks from the product backlog in order to populate the sprint backlog, establishing the deliverables from that iteration. During the cycle's Sprint Execution, active coding and algorithm development takes place. The researchers conduct daily Scrums with alignment meetings to track progress, address bottlenecks, and refine the performance of SynTutor's logic. Within each sprint, Sprint Automation incorporates automated testing workflows to verify that the Linguistic Constraint Matrix produces zero regressions and zero unrated edge types, and that the rule suites, licensing tests, and gold-corpus audit remain green. At the end of each iteration, a Sprint Review Meeting is held to demonstrate the working software to the research adviser to evaluate its functionality. This is immediately followed by a Sprint Retrospective where the team evaluates the development process and identifies operational improvements before moving into the next cycle.
The continuous cycle ultimately culminates in producing a usable software, resulting in a deployed web application wherein learners can input sentences, explore interactive syntax trees, and receive deterministic and real time explanations of grammatical concepts.
3.2 CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK
	Figure 3.2.1 System Architecture of SynTutor
		
Figure 3.2.1 features a pipeline-based structure that processes user-submitted English text through distinct stages, from raw input to interactive, explainable feedback. This framework addresses a key issue with current Grammar Error Correction (GEC) tools. They often fail to explain why a sentence is grammatically incorrect. As a result, learners rely on automated corrections instead of building real skills. SynTutor resolves this by separating *detection* from *explanation*: a trained acceptability channel detects whether a sentence reads as acceptable English, while a deterministic, hand-authored rule matrix explains *why* a sentence is wrong and how it can be repaired.
The pipeline has seven connected components within the SynTutor system. spaCy and benepar_en3 parse the input. A custom recursive-descent decoder and node builder is utilized for the tree reconstruction. The explanation is performed deterministically by the Linguistic Constraint Matrix, reinforced by a positive-evidence licensing layer. A hybrid acceptability detector and a correction-search channel broaden detection coverage without weakening the deterministic explanation. A User-Driven Re-entry loop completes the cycle.
3.2.1 USER INPUT
	This is the system's entry point. The user submits an English sentence or short passage—no transformation occurs here. The component’s sole function is to receive and pass the raw text downstream. Because all subsequent processing depends on what is submitted here, the quality and complexity of the input directly affect every stage that follows.
3.2.2 PREPROCESSING
	Tokenization, sentence segmentation, Part-of-Speech tagging, case normalization, and noise removal via spaCy’s en_core_web_md model. Acts as the quality gate that converts unstructured text into a clean token sequence before parsing. spaCy also supplies each token's fine-grained Part-of-Speech tag and lemma, which are attached to the rebuilt tree so that morphology-sensitive rules are not limited to the coarse phrase labels the parser emits.
3.2.3 NLP PARSING ENGINE
	The computational center, implemented as a three-stage stack:
Tokenization and POS tagging: spaCy's en_core_web_md model segments the text into sentences and tokens and assigns each token a coarse Part-of-Speech category, producing the aligned token stream that the rest of the pipeline consumes.
Constituency parsing: The benepar_en3 neural constituency parser produces a parenthesized phrase-structure parse string for each sentence. It is a fixed, pre-trained model, not a generative model, and it emits phrase-structure trees directly.
Parse-string decoding: A custom recursive-descent decoder converts the parenthesized parse string into a serializable node graph of nested constituents. Each node carries token-coordinate spans aligned against the token stream, and the decoder enforces a span-alignment guard that must match the sentence's token count exactly.
3.2.4 SELECTED SYNTAX TREE
	A single accepted tree, produced by the neural parser and validated for well-formedness. It spans from the root S node through phrase categories (NP, VP, PP) to terminal words. Because it is reconstructed and validated rather than generatively guessed, every downstream XAI judgment rests on a verified structure.
3.2.5 ERROR DETECTION AND XAI
	Implemented by the Linguistic Constraint Matrix (server/matrix.py together with the rule inventory in server/ruleset.py), which deterministically evaluates every parent-child edge. For each edge E(v_d, v_p):
valid if (v_d → v_p) is licensed. Maps the node to a role explanation template.
error if it violates constraint δ. Returns a specific rule-based message and repair suggestion.
unlisted if no rule covers it. Recorded by the gold-corpus audit for future refinement.
The matrix covers subject-verb agreement, determiner-noun number agreement, auxiliary and verb-form selection, clause finiteness, modifier ordering, pronoun case, article selection, and noun plural morphology. Because the edge evaluation is a function of the parsed structure, identical inputs always produce identical verdicts; the deterministic feedback path consults no generative model.
The explanation core is reinforced by two deterministic additions. First, a positive-evidence licensing layer (server/licensing.py) requires every open-class word to be a form the lexicon actually licenses for the Part-of-Speech tag the parser assigned it, so that absence of a rule hit is no longer confused with grammaticality; it also implements the relative-clause rules (2.7 and 5.4) and isolates every checker so that a single failure degrades the verdict rather than silently discarding all findings. Second, when the rules stay clean but a sentence still reads as unacceptable, a hybrid acceptability channel (a CoLA-fine-tuned RoBERTa classifier in server/detector.py) flags the sentence, and an optional masked-language-model correction search (server/corrector.py) locates the single-token swap that restores acceptability. The corrected sentence is re-parsed and must pass the rule matrix before the finding is promoted, and the two words must be provably two forms of one lemma, so a flat meaning change can never be turned into a condemnation. The matrix therefore produces an explicit three-state verdict — grammatical, ungrammatical, or unverified — and never fabricates an explanation it cannot justify with a rule.
3.2.6 OUTPUT GENERATION
	The Output Generation component translates the structured diagnostic results from the previous stage into plain language that the learner can read and use. This means turning error messages, reports of constraint violations, and node role mappings into clear, simple explanations that are appropriate for the user.
This stage serves as the system's pedagogical translation layer. Its output is not just a corrected sentence—it is an instructional response that explains how grammar works, points out specific mistakes, and puts corrections in the context of English syntax rules. This directly supports SynTutor's main goal, which is to help people understand grammar, not just give them corrected output.
When the XAI Structural Validation module finds that every parent-child relationship satisfies the grammatical constraints (vd → vp ∈ C for all nodes) and the licensing layer can account for every word, the system enters its no-error pathway. Instead of generating error messages, it activates positive explanation templates that explicitly confirm the validity of each structure and describe the grammatical roles fulfilled by each constituent. Output Generation then produces a clear confirmation message together with the complete interactive syntax tree. When the verdict is unverified — the rules are clean but the acceptability channel rejects the sentence — the system reports that the rules could not explain the rejection rather than condemning the sentence. This design ensures that learners receive constructive feedback on correct sentences and honest feedback on uncertain ones, reinforcing correct syntactic knowledge rather than remaining silent when no corrections are needed.
3.2.7 USER INTERFACE
	Renders the serialized node graph as a navigable phrase-structure tree and displays the XAI explanations. Learners click nodes to query roles and relationships, and the tree colours each node and edge by its validation status. The interface also exposes a practice module — the Matrix Quiz — that drills the same rules the matrix encodes. It is also the origin of the User-Driven Re-entry loop back to User Input, preserving learner control and preventing dependence on automated correction.
3.3 ALGORITHMS
The SynTutor system combines a trained neural parser with a set of deterministic algorithms: neural constituency parsing, recursive-descent parse-string decoding, the deterministic constraint-matrix evaluation that provides the XAI reasoning, a positive-evidence lexical-licensing algorithm, and a hybrid acceptability-detection and correction-search layer. The trained channels decide *whether* an input is acceptable and *where* a mismatch sits; the deterministic algorithms decide *why*, and produce the teaching explanation. These components work together to transform raw text into a learning tool by processing structural dependencies. Each component handles a specific task and ensures that there is linguistic accuracy being presented without the “black-box” aspect most common grammar tools exhibit.
The parser is a neural constituency model (benepar). It resolves the ambiguity of English syntax by ranking candidate phrase-structure parses and returning the single highest-scoring tree for the sentence, so that a word's role — for example, whether a form is a verb heading a Verb Phrase or a noun heading a Noun Phrase — is fixed before any rule is applied. The decoder then turns that bracketed tree into a node graph with exact token spans. The constraint matrix then evaluates every parent-child edge deterministically, and the licensing layer confirms that every open-class word is a form the lexicon sanctions for the tag it received.
The XAI structural validation works alongside the system’s parsing engine, creating a transparent feedback that identifies grammatical relationships while explaining their correctness to the user. The explanations happen automatically with the deterministic mapping of the tree’s nodes, wherein results will appear as annotations on the side of the syntax tree. Through this, learners gain a learning tool for understanding sentence construction as conveniently presented in SynTutor.

  The neural parser and the deterministic rule matrix create a structured parsing pipeline: the parser fixes the most probable syntax tree while the matrix, reinforced by licensing, validates and explains it. These work together with the hybrid detection channel, resulting in an intuitive program with transparent explanations for learning linguistics.
3.3.1 NEURAL CONSTITUENCY PARSING (FORMALIZATION)
Human language is inherently ambiguous (e.g., prepositional phrase attachment ambiguity, where "I saw the man with the telescope" yields multiple valid syntax trees). To computationally determine the correct hierarchical structure, SynTutor models English syntax with a neural constituency parser rather than a hand-authored grammar.
Formally, let the input sentence be a sequence of tokens w = (w₁, w₂, …, wₙ). A preprocessing function P maps the raw string to a token sequence together with a coarse Part-of-Speech tag for each token:
	P(text) = ((w₁, t₁), (w₂, t₂), …, (wₙ, tₙ))
where tᵢ is the Penn-style tag spaCy assigns to token wᵢ. A (non-generative) parser function Φ then assigns a single constituent tree T over that token sequence:
	Φ(w, t) = T = argmax over candidate trees of score(T | w)
Selected structure. The candidate tree returned by the parser spans from the root S node through phrase categories (NP, VP, PP, …) down to the terminal words, and is emitted as a parenthesized phrase-structure string. The key property SynTutor relies on is the same one a probabilistic model would provide: the parser returns one best, well-formed, S-rooted constituent tree that every downstream stage can trust.
In practical terms within the SynTutor system, this single-tree selection is what prevents the system from generating nonsensical diagrams. Because the structure is fixed before any rule fires, the system guarantees that a word like "bark" in the sentence "The dog barks" is evaluated as a verb attached to a Verb Phrase (VP) rather than as a noun referring to tree bark. The neural parser translates abstract linguistic ambiguity into one definite structural foundation, ensuring the structure is accurate before the Explainable AI (XAI) evaluates it. The parser is a fixed, pre-trained model: it is applied, not trained, at inference time, and identical serialized input always yields the same tree.
3.3.2 PARSE-STRING DECODING AND SPAN ALIGNMENT
Because the parser emits a bracketed string rather than a data structure, SynTutor reconstructs the tree with a recursive-descent decoder. The decoder treats the parenthesized parse as a small formal language over three token kinds — open parenthesis, close parenthesis, and non-parenthesis symbols — recognized with the regular expression:
	( | ) | [^ \s ( )]+
A recursive procedure then reads one node at a time: on "(", it consumes the following symbol as the node's phrase label and parses each child until the matching ")"; a bare symbol becomes a terminal leaf. A node with exactly one child (a unary chain) is collapsed to that child, so the tree contains no redundant single-branch nodes. The decoder raises a failure if it meets a trailing token or a malformed structure, preventing a silently corrupted tree.
The NodeBuilder then assigns each node a deterministic identity and geometry:
a pre-order id (n0, n1, …);
for a leaf, its Part-of-Speech tag as its type, its surface word, and a one-token span [i, i+1);
for an internal node, its phrase label as its type, the space-joined surface of its leaves, and a span [first, last) computed as the union of its children's spans;
a punctuation flag for terminal tags in the punctuation set ( . , : ; ? ! `` '' -RRB- -LRB- ).
Alignment guard. After building, the number of consumed leaves must equal the sentence's token count; otherwise the decoder raises a failure. This guard guarantees that every node's token-coordinate span lines up exactly with spaCy's token stream, which the front end depends on to map a learner's caret position and node clicks to the right words. The result is a serializable graph of nodes, edges, parent-child dependencies, and depth levels — the direct blueprint for the interactive canvas.
3.3.3 DETERMINISTIC CONSTRAINT-MATRIX VALIDATION (XAI)
Once the parser and decoder have produced the data structure, the XAI module operates on top of the generated tree. The XAI does not use generative guessing; it uses a deterministic algorithmic mapping based on the structural dependencies calculated during the parse.
Let V be the set of all nodes in the generated syntax tree T, and let every non-root node v be paired with its parent node v_p. The system evaluates every parent-child edge E(v_d, v_p) against a hand-authored Linguistic Constraint Matrix, denoted as C, whose entries are deterministic functions of the parent and child Part-of-Speech classes. The relationship-evaluation function determines the system's output:
Valid state execution. If the edge (v_d, v_p) is licensed by C, the edge is marked valid and the system extracts the properties of v_d and v_p and maps them to a pedagogical explanation template. For example, subject-verb agreement is confirmed by comparing the number/person key of the subject head with the verb's form; a match yields a role explanation naming the subject label and the verb form.
Error state execution. If the edge violates a constraint in C (e.g., a determiner whose number requirement conflicts with its noun, or a subject and predicate that disagree), the system records the specific constraint identifier (a C-* code) representing the syntactic rule violation and interpolates the exact structural conflict into a rule-based message, together with a repair suggestion derived from the same deterministic lexicon and morphology helpers.
Every edge is therefore classified into exactly one of three states — valid, error, or unlisted — and the sentence summary is computed by a small set of deterministic counters: the number of validated, violated, and unrated edges, the set of distinct constraints that fired, and a final three-state verdict (grammatical when nothing fired and every word licensed, ungrammatical when a violation exists, and unverified when a word or checker could not be confirmed). Because evaluation is a single traversal in which each edge is decided in constant time, the pass runs in linear time, O(|V|), over the tree — fast enough to explain a sentence in real time.
This exact mathematical linkage between the parser's output and the XAI's deterministic evaluation ensures that all educational feedback provided by SynTutor is computationally verifiable, structurally sound, and entirely free from "black-box" hallucinations.
By structuring the algorithm as a sequential pipeline—where the neural parser establishes the definite structure and the XAI evaluates constraints against that exact structure—SynTutor achieves full transparency. Unlike commercial grammar tools that rely on opaque sequence-to-sequence prediction, this deterministic approach ensures that every piece of feedback provided to the learner is directly anchored to a visible, verifiable node in the syntax tree.
3.3.4 POSITIVE-EVIDENCE LEXICAL LICENSING
A rule matrix alone is a blacklist: a sentence is "valid" whenever no hand-written rule fires, which makes the absence of a rule indistinguishable from grammaticality. The licensing algorithm closes this gap by demanding positive evidence for every open-class word.
For each open-class leaf (nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs) with surface s and parser-assigned tag t, the algorithm converts the Penn tag to a universal Part-of-Speech class and queries the deterministic lexicon (lemminflect) for the lemma and inflectional paradigm. A word is licensed when its surface is a listed inflection of some lemma under the tagged class. When it is not, the algorithm tests whether the surface looks like a mis-built inflection of a word the lexicon does know, by stripping a suffix family (plural -s/-es/-ies, verbal -ed/-d/-ing, comparative -er/-est, with undoubling and y→i handling) and checking whether a compatible lemma exists; a match marks the form malformed (e.g., "runned", "childs", "goed"), while an unknown word is marked unverified rather than condemned. A curated high-frequency list prevents clean sentences from being over-flagged. The layer also implements the relative-clause rules by walking SBAR/WHNP structures to check subject-gap agreement and the who/which/whose choice. Crucially, each checker runs in isolation, so one crashing checker degrades the verdict to unverified instead of erasing every other finding. The output of this stage is a tri-state result per word and an overall lexical-coverage ratio.
3.3.5 HYBRID ACCEPTABILITY DETECTION AND CORRECTION SEARCH
The final algorithmic layer broadens coverage without compromising explainability by combining a trained detector with a deterministic correction search.
Detection. A CoLA-fine-tuned RoBERTa acceptability classifier (server/detector.py) decides whether the sentence reads as acceptable English, giving broad coverage without needing a hand-written rule per error. The detector is optional and degrades gracefully: if its weights cannot be loaded, the system falls back to the rules-only verdict.
Combination. A sentence is grammatical only if both channels accept it. When the matrix reports a violation, the verdict is ungrammatical and the model's view is shown alongside it. When the matrix is clean but the model rejects the sentence, the rules cannot explain the rejection, so the verdict is set to unverified and the rejection is surfaced instead of a fabricated rule explanation.
Correction search. To explain a rules-clean rejection deterministically, the system performs a masked-language-model sweep in text space (server/corrector.py): it masks one word at a time, asks a masked-language model (distilbert-base-uncased by default) for top-k replacements, and substitutes each candidate back into the acceptability model until a swap flips the sentence from unacceptable to acceptable. That single-token swap is the error locus (the bad word) and the candidate correction (the good word). The corrected sentence is then re-parsed and must pass the rule matrix cleanly, which supplies the authoritative required Part-of-Speech tag for the good word; finally, a morphological diff (lemminflect) requires the two words to share a lemma. Only when all gates pass is the finding promoted to a deterministic ungrammatical verdict, under the constraint identifier C-MLM-ANOMALY, with an explanation generated from a fixed template rather than a language model. A promotion therefore never rewrites a mere meaning change into a grammar condemnation, and the deterministic explanation path remains free of generative text.
3.4 OPERATIONAL FRAMEWORK
The operational framework of SynTutor delineates the functional execution of the system from the initial user interaction to the final delivery of pedagogical feedback. The system is architected upon a dynamic, event-driven Input-Process-Output (IPO) model, shown in Figure 3.4 below. In contrast to traditional static grammar checkers that execute a single linear scan and terminate, the operational flow of SynTutor utilizes a continuous feedback loop. It maintains an active, stateful listening mode, which allows user interactions to trigger secondary and tertiary processing states without necessitating a full system reload.


Figure 3.4.1 IPO Framework of SynTutor

3.4.1 THE INPUT PHASE
The Input phase is categorized into two distinct interaction stages, each responsible for transmitting specific data payloads to the processing engine. The primary stage involves textual input and sanitization, wherein the user accesses the client-side interface to input a raw string of English text. To ensure system stability and parsing efficiency, the interface applies an input validation protocol, stripping the string of non-standard characters and evaluating the string length to prevent memory saturation during the parsing phase. Subsequently, the user selects a specific target sentence, effectively isolating the input scope. This selected string is then bundled into an API request payload and transmitted to the backend middleware.
Following the initial syntax visualization, the system transitions into the secondary stage, characterized by asynchronous event-driven inputs. In this state, the frontend listens for user mouse events. A click on a terminal leaf node, representing an individual word, or a non-terminal parent node, representing a phrase cluster, triggers an event handler. This handler packages the specific node's unique identifier, lexical type, and hierarchical coordinate data into a localized JSON payload, which is then passed back to the processing engine for targeted evaluation.
3.4.2 THE PROCESS PHASE
The Process phase constitutes the core linguistic and deterministic engine of the system. It translates the raw string inputs and event triggers into structured, verifiable data through a rigorous three-stage sequential pipeline. Initially, the system conducts lexical and morphological analysis. Upon receiving the validated target sentence, a pre-processing script is executed to perform tokenization, systematically dividing the string into discrete word tokens and handling punctuation delimiters. Following tokenization, a Part-of-Speech (POS) tagger is applied to assign a preliminary lexical category to each token based on its surface-level morphology and immediate surrounding context. This step prepares the data array for deep structural parsing.
The second stage involves syntactic parsing, tree reconstruction, and serialization. The tagged token array is fed into the Natural Language Processing (NLP) middleware, where the benepar neural constituency parser produces the highest-scoring phrase-structure parse string for the sentence, and the recursive-descent decoder rebuilds it into a hierarchical node graph. The decoder assigns each node a deterministic identifier, a Part-of-Speech or phrase type, a token-coordinate span, and its parent-child dependencies, and enforces a span-alignment guard so that the number of leaves matches the sentence's token count exactly. The serialized nodes, edges, and depth levels are then transmitted to the frontend.
The third stage encompasses the Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) structural evaluation together with the hybrid verification channel. The XAI module validates every parent-child relationship against the system's predefined Linguistic Constraint Matrix (C) and the positive-evidence licensing layer, and it compiles a valid response when a relationship is licensed or an error response (identifying the exact rule violation) when it is not. A trained acceptability classifier then reviews the sentence: if the matrix is clean but the model rejects the input, a correction search locates the single-token repair, re-parses the corrected sentence, and only promotes the finding once the corrected sentence passes the matrix and the morphological gates. The combined result is finalized as a three-state verdict — grammatical, ungrammatical, or unverified.
3.4.3 THE OUTPUT PHASE
The Output phase is responsible for translating the complex, serialized backend data structures into an accessible, interactive educational user interface. This phase delivers two distinct forms of synchronized output. The primary visual output consists of the interactive canvas. The frontend receives the serialized JSON tree data and executes a rendering algorithm to calculate the coordinate geometry for each node. A dynamic, mathematical tree diagram is rendered on the interface, illustrating the hierarchical syntactic and dependency relations connecting the words. To facilitate the secondary input loop, the canvas is rendered with interactive, scalable hitboxes around every node and branch, and each node and edge is coloured according to its validation status.
The secondary output is the pedagogical feedback generated by the XAI module. Triggered sequentially after the structural evaluation stage, the system outputs a natural-language explanation. This data is injected into the Document Object Model (DOM) and displayed within the user interface as a contextual modal or side-panel. Rather than merely providing a corrected sentence, this output explicitly narrates the structural rules governing the user's selected node, thereby bridging abstract algorithmic logic with human-readable educational instruction.
3.5 USE CASE / FLOWCHART / STATE DIAGRAM
	In this section, three UML diagrams will be utilized to portray how the SynTutor system operates. The three diagrams will act as complementary representations of the system's operation along different dimensions. The Use Case Diagram will show the system's functional area, as seen by the actors who use it. The Flow Chart will document how the system (automated processing path and user for every reason for exiting the system) executes sequentially. The State Diagram will model the individual states of the system and how the various events will provide for the change of the system states.

The three diagrams will provide an integrated and coherent representation of SynTutor's architecture and will reinforce the design principles established through the Conceptual Framework regarding separating automated processes and user-driven processes, how the explanations provided for XAI were precomputed, and how the only way to return to the pipeline was for the user to submit text.
Figure 3.4 Use Case Diagram of SynTutor
		
The Use Case Diagram, presented in Figure 3.4, defines the functional boundaries of SynTutor by identifying the interactions that the system's primary actor — the Student — can perform within the system. Standard UML notation uses a labeled rectangle to show the system boundary which contains all use cases while the actor stands outside this boundary to show their role as an external system behavior initiator. The diagram shows only user-initiated use cases while it excludes internal automated processes which include preprocessing and parsing and syntax tree generation because these processes function as system-level computations that do not involve user interactions.
The Student actor has three main use cases which include Submit text, View syntax tree, and Interact with Syntax Tree. The Student and Submit text relationship creates the first system entry point because all system operations depend on this particular use case to function. The learner can access phrase-structure visualization through the node graph that the neural parser and its recursive-descent decoder produce, according to the View syntax tree association. The learner can use the User Interface to explore tree nodes and examine their grammatical functions and structural connections which the Interact with Syntax Tree function permits.
The dependencies between system boundary use cases are governed by two structural relationships. The first relationship consists of an <<include>> link that connects View syntax tree to View basic explanation because all syntax tree viewings need to create a basic explanation. The relationship shows that the system design requires basic output to include both visual syntax tree and natural-language explanation which the system delivers as a single response after completing the automated pipeline. The <<include>> relationship describes basic explanation because it exists as an essential output element which users cannot access separately. The second is an <<extend>> relationship from Detailed explanation to Interact with Syntax Tree, which indicates that detailed, node-level explanations are optional and conditional. The base use case, Interact with Syntax Tree, is fully functional and self-contained without the extension; the detailed explanation is activated only when the user selects a specific node, which constitutes the guard condition for the extension. The relationship establishes a fundamental architectural principle for SynTutor because it describes how node-level explanations use stored XAI output instead of real-time generation, and users must intentionally select content to access those explanations.






	Figure 3.5 System Flowchart of SynTutor

The Use Case Diagram defines what the system does from the user's perspective, the Flowchart presented in Figure 3.5 defines how the system executes those functions, tracing the full operational flow from text submission through pipeline processing, interactive engagement, and eventual termination or re-entry. The flowchart employs standard notation: ovals represent terminal states (Start and End), rectangles represent process steps, and diamonds represent decision points. The diagram is visually partitioned into two layers — an automated pipeline occupying the central column and a user interaction branch occupying the right column — with color-coded connectors distinguishing system-automated flow from user-triggered behavior and feedback loops.
The automated pipeline starts at its initial Start terminal and moves through its first User submits text terminal which serves as the only entry point for all subsequent processing. The system enters the Preprocessing stage to execute tokenization and part-of-speech tagging and normalization functions which produce a clean structured token sequence. The NLP Parsing Engine receives preprocessed input to execute the benepar neural constituency parser, which returns the highest-scoring phrase-structure parse string, and the recursive-descent decoder rebuilds that string into the selected syntax tree. The decoded node graph serves as the primary evidence base for all upcoming assessments. The Error Detection & XAI stage then applies the XAI Structural Validation module, evaluating every parent-child node relationship in the tree against the predefined linguistic constraint matrix and the positive-evidence licensing layer, and a hybrid acceptability check confirms that the sentence reads as acceptable. The outcome of this evaluation — whether valid-state explanations confirming grammatical correctness or error-state messages identifying specific rule violations — is passed to Display output in UI, which renders both the interactive syntax tree visualization and the basic natural-language explanation simultaneously. This output process does not require any decision gate because the system automatically generates and displays basic explanations according to the Use Case Diagram requirements established by <<include>> relationships.
The process reaches its initial choice point after it displays output. The first choice point occurs when users start interacting with the tree. When users select a node, the process moves to the user interaction section according to the established flow. User selects a node triggers Retrieve XAI, which is explicitly labeled to indicate that no reprocessing occurs at this stage — the node-level explanation is retrieved from the output already computed by the XAI Structural Validation module during the main pipeline execution. The system transfers the acquired information to Display detailed explanation, which enables learners to view both the node's grammatical function and its structural explanation. The interaction loop returns to the first decision point after completion because users can select nodes multiple times during their current session without starting a new pipeline process. The flow reaches its second decision point which determines whether the user submits new text when the user fails to interact with the tree. The system starts the re-entry loop when the user answers yes because it sends the user back to User submits text which marks the start of the complete pipeline process. The system architecture requires that users must start a new processing cycle whenever they submit new text. The End terminal functions as the session endpoint when users provide a negative answer. The two feedback loops are thus functionally and visually distinct: the interaction loop is a lightweight retrieval cycle that operates entirely within the output layer while the re-entry loop is a full pipeline restart initiated exclusively by a deliberate user action.
Figure 3.6 State Diagram of SynTutor

The Flowchart describes the procedural sequence of operations, the State Diagram presented in Figure 3.6 models SynTutor's behavior as a set of discrete, persistent system states and the event-driven transitions between them. The system develops its state-based design because it operates in two main states: active mode and inactive mode. The diagram uses standard UML state machine notation which displays its two pseudostates through filled circles, its named states through rounded rectangles, and its event-driven transitions through labeled arrows.
The system starts from its starting pseudostate and proceeds to its Idle state which shows the system's inactive state until users begin to interact with it. The user input of text activates the system which enters the Processing state that functions as a complete automatic process through its internal sub-machine. The Processing state begins its initial pseudostate which leads to five sub-states that follow each other in this order: Preprocessing and Parsing and Syntax tree generated and Explanation computed and Output ready. The pipeline stages in the Conceptual Framework describe the pipeline stages which start from tokenization and normalization and continue through neural parsing and XAI structural evaluation until reaching the final serialization of the syntax tree with its explanations. The sub-state labels which use sentence spacing establish both readability and compliance with UML naming conventions. The Processing composite state ends when its nested final state indicates that all automated processes have finished running. The system moves to the Viewing state through an Output ready transition which happens after Processing ends.
	The Viewing state outputs interactive system access through its rendered syntax tree and User Interface explanation. The system can exit this state through two different transitions. The system begins to explore syntax nodes when users select them according to the Exploring state which shows learners who search specific tree nodes while getting their XAI explanations. Back to syntax tree serves as the transition between Exploring state and Viewing state because it shows how learners can leave node queries to see the entire tree structure without having to start over. The system returns to Processing composite state when users submit new text in Viewing state because this action starts the entire processing pipeline again. The system design permits users to start new pipeline runs only from Viewing state because the text submission interface exists solely in the main output view. The system design prohibits users from re-entering the system during exploration because it maintains the computational state of the system throughout user sessions. The User exits transition from the Viewing state leads to the UML final state which ends the session.
3.6 RESPONDENTS OF THE STUDY
The respondents of this study are categorized into two distinct groups to ensure a comprehensive and multi-faceted evaluation of the SynTutor system. The evaluation phase requires assessing both the pedagogical impact and usability of the software from a learner's perspective, as well as validating the linguistic accuracy and pedagogical soundness of the Explainable AI (XAI) from an expert's perspective.
The first group of respondents consists of the primary target end-users: Bachelor of Science in Computer Science (BSCS) students enrolled at De La Salle University - Dasmariñas (DLSU-D). Through purposive sampling, a total of fifty (50) student respondents will be selected to participate in the system testing and evaluation. The selection of BSCS students is highly relevant to the study; their technical background allows them to effectively navigate and evaluate the system's Interactive UI Canvas and the dynamic syntax tree generation, while their status as English language learners makes them ideal candidates to test the system's ability to improve grammatical comprehension and syntactic priming. The students will evaluate the system based on its usability, the clarity of the natural-language explanations provided by the Output Generation component, and their perceived improvement in structural grammar analysis.
To qualify for the study, student respondents must meet the following inclusion criteria: (1) currently enrolled as regular BSCS students at DLSU-D, (2) have a fundamental understanding of English grammar, and (3) are willing to interact with the SynTutor web application to analyze English texts.
The second group of respondents consists of expert evaluators, specifically three (3) English and Linguistics professors or instructors. Because SynTutor utilizes a neural constituency parser together with a deterministic constraint matrix and an XAI structural validation module, it is crucial that the generated phrase-structure syntax trees and rule-based diagnostic feedback are linguistically sound. The expert evaluators will be selected using purposive sampling based on their academic credentials, teaching experience in English syntax, and familiarity with language learning technologies.
The role of the expert respondents is to conduct a rigorous quality assurance test on the system's educational output. They will assess the parser's structural precision, verify that the XAI does not produce "hallucinated" or grammatically incorrect feedback, and evaluate whether the system's deterministic mapping correctly translates complex syntactical constraints into pedagogical explanations suitable for students. By combining the feedback from both the student learners and the linguistic experts, the researchers can continuously refine the application through the Agile-Scrum development sprints, ensuring that SynTutor is both technically robust and practically effective as a digital tutoring agent.
3.6.1 Ethical Considerations
To ensure full institutional compliance, data privacy, and protection of the participants’ data, the evaluation of the system adheres to the following protocols:
Informed Consent and Voluntary Participation
Participation of the respondents is strictly voluntary. A written informed consent that outlines the scope of the study, potential risks, and the intended usage of collected data will be given prior to data gathering. Participants retain the right to withdraw at any moment without penalty.
Anonymity & Data Confidentiality
All collected data including syntactic pretest/posttest scores, the SUS survey responses, and the validation of experts are removed from personal identifiable information. Each participant is assigned a code to ensure anonymity in compliance with data privacy standards.
Data Security
All collected digital data will be encrypted and stored in a secure digital repository accessible only to the researchers, to be used for academic purposes only.
3.6.2 Statistical Treatment of Data
In order to measure the effectiveness of the application and its overall user experience, the data collected from the respondents will be processed using the following statistical treatment methods:
Paired Samples t-Test: Evaluates the student learning gains by comparing the mean baseline performance on the syntactic pretest against post-intervention performance on the parallel posttest. If the pretest/posttest score distributions violate the assumption of normality, the Wilcoxon Signed-Rank test will be applied as an alternative.
System Usability Scale (SUS) Scoring: Student feedback on the 10-item Likert scale SUS questionnaire is calculated using standard SUS scoring formulas to produce a normalized score out of 100, evaluating system usability, learnability, and overall user interface navigation.
Mean Scores and Percentage Accuracy: Ratings from the 4-point rubric in the validation matrix are synthesized using descriptive statistics for the accuracy of the generated syntax tree and correctness of the XAI feature.
